417] [17592186555441 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555441 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555441 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b938e797-07a9-546d-9bec-948b3e7a15fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555716 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "980312ed-2719-5060-9fbb-108e85ad677e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555850 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555850 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555850 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555850 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2-2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555850 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4f61444-c8ba-5ff9-98c2-7150307d9838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555850 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t+2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2(-1)+2)+(1-1)\\cdot (2(1)+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+2,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(2t+2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(-1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(2(1)+2)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =((-1)-1)\\cdot (2-2(-1))+(1-1)\\cdot (2-2(1)) \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
